 { Loyd, 1860 Rook's pawns pose more difficulties for the defence. This is logical: one of the stopping diagonals can be very short, or even disappear when the pawn reaches the 7th rank. This case arises quite often, and it contains an unexpected resource which is worth knowing. Black threatens to place his king on g1 and his knight on g2 to close the only stopping diagonal. White's position looks hopeless; however, there is a hidden resource. } 1. Bd7 $1 { White must force the pawn to the 7th rank to apply the drawing procedure. } ( 1. Bc6 Kg1 2. Bd5 Ng2 $19 { and the pawn promotes. } ) 
h2 2. Bc6+ Kg1 ( 2... Nf3 3. Ke2 $11 { . } ) 
3. Bh1 $1 { Only move; the threat was 3... Ng2. } Kxh1 ( 3... Ng2 4. Ke2 Nf4 5. Ke1 $11 { does not work either. Now the black king cannot leave the corner due to the knight 's inherent inability to lose time. But where do we move the white king now ? There is a rule to simplify our calculation: we must move the king to a square of the same colour as the knight; thus the knight will be able to check the king, but not to control the opposite-coloured square. } ) 
4. Kf2 $1 { Now the black king is trapped. Instead } ( 4. Kf1 $2 Nf5 5. Kf2 Ng3 $19 { leads to a quick zugzwang. } ) 
Nf3 5. Kf1 Nd2+ 6. Kf2 Ne4+ 7. Kf1 { A brilliant and impressive study by Sam Loyd which can make us wrongly think that this ending is always a draw with a rook's pawn. This is only true when the defending king is close enough. Let us have a look at the following example. } 
 1/2-1/2

