 { When the pawn is less advanced, zugzwang is impossible if the defending king and knight control the square in front of the pawn (the opposite colour to the bishop) . Then a type of position arises which is interesting only when the position of the knight is unstable. Here zugzwang can help the analysis, but it is impossible to set a general rule. Each case requires calculation. Therefore, we present just one example. It can be a guide for further study, but not a source of theoretical conclusions. V. Bron, 1955 } { _ } 1. Bb3+ Kc5 { If } ( 1... Ke5 2. Be6 { and zugzwang. } ) 
2. Ba2 { Other bishop moves also win; instead, } ( 2. Ke6 $2 { allows the knight to relocate, which would make zugzwang impossible. } Nh7 $1 3. Ke7 Nf8 $11 { and the black king just moves around the pawn. } 4. Bc2 Kc6 { and now: } 5. Bb1 ( 5. Ba4 Kc5 6. Be8 Kd5 7. Bf7 Kc6 8. Bh5 Kc5 $11 { not } ( 8. Ba2 Kd5 $2 9. Bf3 Ke5 10. Be4 $18 { zugzwang. } ) 
 ) 
Kd5 $1 ( 5. Ba2 Kc5 6. Be4 { zugzwang. } ) 
6. Bd3 Kc6 $11 ) 
Kc6 ( 2... Ng4 3. Bb1 Nf6 4. Be4 $18 Ng8 5. Kd7 Nf6 6. Ke6 { as in the final position. } ) 
3. Ke6 Nh7 ( 3... Kc5 4. Bb1 { or } ( 3... Nh7 3. Bd5 Nh7 5. Ke7 ) 
 ) 
4. Bd5+ Kc5 5. Ke7 Nf6 ( 5... Nf8 6. Be4 $18 { is another zugzwang position, also winning. } ) 
6. Bf3 Ng8+ 7. Ke6 Nf6 8. Be4 $1 { Zugzwang! In this type of position, the only useful conclusion is that no theoretical rules can be established. Accurate calculation is essential, and searching zugzwang positions is very useful. } 
